# Tasks: TASK-STATE-MACHINE

Purpose:
  Define the 10-state task lifecycle for the AI autonomous execution loop.
  A task is no longer a static TODO — it is a state machine that progresses
  through well-defined phases. States drive execution; sub-states handle
  failure/retry cycles within a phase.

Owns:
  Task lifecycle states
  State transitions
  Sub-state transitions
  Progress recording

States:

  DISCOVERING    — Task goal understood, context being gathered
  PLANNING       — Plan synthesized from context and knowledge
  IMPLEMENTING   — Code, tests, or artifacts being produced
  TESTING        — Work is under validation (unit, integration, BDD)
  REVIEWING      — Code, architecture, and rules under human-system review
  FIXING         — Deficiencies identified, remediation in progress
  DOCUMENTING    — Human-readable documentation generated
  LEARNING       — Knowledge extracted, consolidated, and promoted
  COMPLETED      — Task fully done (Engineering Done, not just Functional Done)
  BLOCKED        — Cannot proceed: explicit blocker declared
  ESCALATED      — Transferred to human; requires external input

SubStates:

  TESTING:
    state: TESTING
    sub_states:
      PASSED   — tests green, proceed to REVIEWING
      FAILED   — tests red, transition to FIXING

  FIXING:
    state: FIXING
    sub_states:
      IN_PROGRESS — fix being applied
      READY       — fix applied, ready to RETURN to TESTING

  REVIEWING:
    state: REVIEWING
    sub_states:
      PASS               — all review criteria met
      PASS_WITH_WARNINGS — passed but warnings noted
      CHANGES_REQUIRED   — fixes must be applied, return to FIXING
      BLOCKED            — review finding blocks progress
      ESCALATE           — review finding requires human decision

Transitions:

  Main flow:
    DISCOVERING -> PLANNING -> IMPLEMENTING -> TESTING -> REVIEWING ->
    DOCUMENTING -> LEARNING -> COMPLETED

  Testing failure:
    TESTING -> FAILED -> FIXING -> TESTING
    (repeat until tests pass or loop guards trigger)

  Reviewing failure:
    REVIEWING -> CHANGES_REQUIRED -> FIXING -> TESTING -> REVIEWING

  Blocked:
    ANY_STATE -> BLOCKED  (requires explicit blocker declaration)
    BLOCKED -> previous_state  (when blocker resolved)

  Escalated:
    ANY_STATE -> ESCALATED  (requires explicit escalation reason)
    ESCALATED is terminal until human intervention

ProgressRule:

  Each iteration within a cycle (TESTING -> FAILED -> FIXING -> TESTING)
  MUST record:
    BEFORE:    measurable state before action
    CHANGE:    what was changed
    AFTER:     measurable state after action
    RESULT:    outcome (pass/fail)
    PROGRESS:  yes / no

  Example:
    BEFORE:
      3 tests failing
    CHANGE:
      moved lock acquisition into transaction boundary
    AFTER:
      1 test failing
    RESULT:
      tests failed
    PROGRESS:
      yes

Rules:

  [TM1] A task MUST occupy exactly one primary state at any time.
  [TM2] The TESTING -> FAILED -> FIXING -> TESTING cycle MUST NOT be
        infinite — loop guards in LOOP-PROTECTION.sdd apply.
  [TM3] Each iteration in a failure cycle MUST record progress evidence
        (BEFORE / CHANGE / AFTER / RESULT / PROGRESS).
  [TM4] AI MUST NOT claim "tests passed" without a measurable BEFORE/AFTER
        delta showing progress.
  [TM5] BLOCKED state MUST declare an explicit blocker with type and ref.
  [TM6] ESCALATED state is terminal — task remains in ESCALATED until
        human action resolves it.
  [TM7] COMPLETED requires Engineering Done, not Functional Done
        (bax DONE-PROOF.sdd).
  [TM8] REVIEWING is embedded inside the execution loop — it is NOT a
        separate post-completion step.
  [TM9] State history MUST be append-only — past transitions are immutable.
  [TM10] The LEARNING state MUST produce at least one knowledge artifact
         (skill, decision, or lesson) before COMPLETED.

